 keep rebooting  their universe all the darn time and it  was the impetus for the initial  crisis on Infinite Earths look we've got  all these multiverses we're not getting  new readers at the rate we would like  because people are intimidated by all  this stuff let's simplify it so it makes  better sense and we'll get new people  and it and again that worked for a while  until they felt it got too complicated  and they did fine  Oh crisis and they did these other sort  of events that we're tweaking until they  finally rebooted it after flashpoint and  now they're doing rebirth and they just  you have to keep cycling through because  new audiences are an oddly finite  resource theoretically should be an  infinite resource as a new generation  people become old enough to consume your  entertainment you should never run out  of new readers in theory the thing is  though is that intimidation factor that  I talked about I do think is part of the  reason that say in the last decade or so  a lot of very long-running soap operas  have been slowly dying and getting  canceled and I think the reason for that  is eventually new people stopped coming  in or if they don't stop altogether the  growth of new readers new audience  coming in eventually is smaller than the  older audience either deciding they've  had enough or if it's been around long  enough dying off and that just gets you  diminishing returns unless you can find  a way to really fire up supercharge the  new audience and Marvel Cinematic  Universe may be hitting that point soon  because when the Marvel Cinematic  Universe started you had your initial  core group of superheroes building up to  the Avengers now that all worked great  phase one really kind of flawless in  terms of execution I'm not saying the  movies themselves were perfect I'm  saying in terms of structure how they  set it up could each individual film  while connected to the others did not  require you to have watch the others and  I where you can even watch that first  Avengers movie without having seen those  other ones it catches you up well enough  so even the big event one that was a  culmination of all these people could  just jump on there and it was fine but  as we've gone along since then because  they want all the events not only to tie  into each other but to be consequential  to each other so that the events matter  then it makes things become more and  more intertwined and more tightly knit  and it becomes more and more necessary  for people who are very serious about  this to watch everything now  you're a complete geek like I am you are  more than happy to do that but if you  are a mainstream audience and let us be  honest the complete geek audience like  me  we are not enough to maintain this  franchise on our own this has to bring  in mainstream audiences and I think  after infinity war we are at risk for  them starting to become just overwhelmed  by how much back history there is that  they're not caught up on or they didn't  watch you know the latest Thor because  they don't like Thor as much as they'd  like Captain America now suddenly  there's a whole chunk of stuff going to  infinity war that they're not going to  cut up on they're going to be going what  the heck is going on I think there is  potentially depending on how its  executed a certain amount of backlash  that if any war might draw from  mainstream more casual audiences which  is the audience that has made this a  billion-dollar franchise instead of just  a several hundred million dollar  franchise now again everything I'm  talking about here is separate from this  concept of superhero fatigue which is  something that gets tossed out around  can I'd honestly don't think that's a  thing I do think continuity fatigue  could very well be a thing again for  mainstream audiences who are not willing  to put in the investment to watch  everything or to read a wiki on whatever  they weren't willing to watch in the  theater or on Netflix or whatever and  the bigger this universe gets the more  intertwined geeks like me wanted to get  but that is potentially alienating to  the mainstream customers and that is  where things might start to diminish now  this is not necessarily a foregone  conclusion there are ways to mitigate it  I think the smartest thing that Marvel  could do to help stave this off is that  following infinity war is to basically  start over  to go back to smaller less continuity  driven stories for the individual  franchises they need to go back to the  roots of those initial you know two or  three films that were given to  characters like Captain America  like Iron Man like Thor and then have  them be more so contained like has the  references to the other movies in this  universe be Easter eggs not plot central  important points and most importantly  not try and Trump or one-up infinity war  in terms of scale or intertwining or the  coming together of all the characters  for a long time because they are not  going to be able to top at anytime soon  and infinity war could be great I hope  it will be but again I don't believe in  superhero fatigue I do believe in events  fatigue because we've seen it in the  comics themselves I have known more than  a few friends who got really tired of  the continued big events that were just  continuity tweaks or reboots in DC  Marvel has really been overdoing events  on the comics lately they've had like  one a year for a while I think they even  had to in a year like not that long ago  I can't even keep up with all this and  it's a saying that these publishers or  in this case in the case the movies  these Studios tend to get wrong which is  that they look at all the money that's a  big event one like the Avengers pulled  in and go great will do big event ones  but the thing is the big event only  works on the foundation of each  individual franchise which needs to be  on solid footing in the first place or  to bring it into the terms of comics the  big events in comics character deaths  new and new characters introduced  crossovers people turning evil stuff  like that was really only impactful when  it wasn't the norm and it wasn't  happening every other freaking issue  when the loudest thing you have becomes  the normal then that's it everything  just becomes white noise as opposed to  being a peak to a slower standard volume  now this is a case where the realities  of moviemaking  might basically force Marvel films and  Marvel Studios to do the smarter thing  and that is that these actors are not  going to play these parts brother  forever they're either going to age out  of them or they're just going to decide  on their own you know what I've had my  fill and that will force them to  basically retire these characters  characters like Tony Stark and Captain  America  eventually they'll retire some of the  other long runners like Thor and Loki  and all these others and it will force  them to actually pay attention into the  new generation of heroes and like I said  basically start over and I think that  will be a very good thing I think  if basically if actors didn't age we'd  be in a ton of trouble it would just  keep escalating and escalating and  escalating but the realities of  filmmaking are going to force them to do  what they never do in the comics which  is too rich is to slow down back up and  reassess as opposed to keep trying to  one-up all the time because continuity  is fun it's great it's wonderful for  guys like me but like I said guys like  me do not make up a big enough chunk of  the audience to sustain these movies and  continuity can be a big headache for  standard viewers and continuity can also  become just very self aggrandizing where  you're basically padding you know the  longtime fans on the back for noticing  stuff going thanks for sticking with us  here's a cookie but you're not actually  moving things forward in terms of  gaining a new audience and keeping the  thing fresh and revitalized because  continuity cinematic universes haven't  been I think a wonderful thing and I  don't want to see it just come apart at  the seams because no one's paying  attention to the eventual problems no  one's doing the routine maintenance on  the car and no one you know swapping out  parts in the engine when they need to  and I don't want them to just drive the  car into the ground you know to go back  to the analogy of the machine that I  only partially understand so those are  my thoughts on what might be the  eventual demise of the Marvel Cinematic  Universe and by extension the impossible  inevitable demise of any successful  cinematic universe and possibly how to  if not stop it at least delay it so what  are your thoughts on all of this  whatever they are drop something down in  the comments let's talk about it don't  forget to like subscribe check me out on  Twitter add council of geeks give a  listen to the council of geeks podcast  available on iTunes and stitcher I am  also one half of the punch like a girl  podcast also available on iTunes and  stitcher and it's a  next time this council is adjourned  [Applause]  [Applause]
 

